.TH POPLABTEST 1
.SH NAME
poplabtest \- check every Danby population model written as a poplab(2) script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B poplabtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Poplabtest
is to
.IR poplab (2)
what
.IR mechlabtest (1)
is to
.IR mechlab (2):
it reads the
.B SPEC
constant out of every model under
.B /appl/danby
that calls
.BR poplab->run ,
so that what is checked is the string that ships rather than a copy of
it, and then checks that the spec parses, that every parameter's default
lies inside its range, that no derivative is a NaN at the model's own
initial state, and that the model has the number of populations its
curriculum page describes.
.PP
Four models carry a property of their own, moved here from
.B temple/danbytest
along with the models themselves.
.B Childcare
must have every rate zero when every population is zero.
.B Fishingone
must be at rest at its bionomic equilibrium \(em the stock at which the
price of a catch just covers its cost, and the effort that holds it
there.
.B Diseasebirthdeath1
balances births against deaths exactly, so its three rates must sum to
zero, and
.B diseasebirthdeath2
does not, so its rates must sum to the birth rate less the natural death
rate less what the disease kills.
.PP
.B -v
lists each model with its parameter and state counts.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/poplabtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mechlabtest (1),
.IR poplab (2),
.IR dynsys (2)
.SH BUGS
The state counts are a table in this program rather than something the
spec states, so adding a model means adding a line here or its count goes
unchecked.
